{"duration_api_ms":84185,"stop_reason":"end_turn","session_id":"03e66157-b933-45a3-b5e9-0b0ff7504c45","total_cost_usd":0.30512380000000006,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11033,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"output_tokens":10828,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":6568},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11033,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":10828,"cache_read_input_tokens":1459,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11033,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11033},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"modelUsage":{"claude-opus-5-5":{"inputTokens":2,"outputTokens":10828,"cacheReadInputTokens":1459,"cacheCreationInputTokens":11033,"webSearchRequests":0,"costUSD":0.30512380000000006,"contextWindow":1000000,"maxOutputTokens":128000,"thinkingTokens":6568,"canonicalModel":"claude-opus-5-5","provider":"firstParty","costBasis":"list"}},"permission_denials":[],"terminal_reason":"completed","fast_mode_state":"off","fast_mode_disabled_reason":"sdk_opt_in_required","subagent_stats":{"spawned":0,"requested":{"background":0,"foreground":0,"unset":0},"started_in_background":0,"max_depth":0,"spawned_by_subagents":0,"completed":0,"failed":0,"killed":{"parent":0,"user":0,"system":0},"refused":{"depth_limit":0,"concurrency_limit":0,"budget":0},"by_type":{}},"is_error":false,"num_turns":1,"subtype":"success","api_error_status":null,"result":"{\"merged_document\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\\n\\nAlthough Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years. In fact, its encounter with Jupiter was optimized in part to ensure that future planetary flybys would be possible.\\n\\nThe Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\\n\\nThe first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2 began its long-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth. Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions. Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\\n\\nDuring its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\\n\\nThe spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 mph) and evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (497 miles) below the top cloud surface. Its rings were found to be extremely variable in thickness and transparency.\\n\\nVoyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons. In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly nine-year travels. Images of the moon showed a strange object whose surface was a mishmash of peculiar features that seemed to have no rhyme or reason. Uranus itself appeared generally featureless.\\n\\nThe spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"decisions\":[{\"slot\":\"title\",\"candidates\":[{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_a.md\"},{\"text\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"document\":\"source_b.md\"}],\"chosen\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Both documents carry the same title; the base's copy is kept.\"}],\"dispositions\":[{\"segment\":\"a2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft name and encounter count.\"},{\"segment\":\"a4\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"reason\":\"Fixed 'Ur anus' typo; corrected next target, spacecraft and study time.\"},{\"segment\":\"a5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2 began its long-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected spacecraft, observation type and start date.\"},{\"segment\":\"a6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected light-level factor.\"},{\"segment\":\"a7\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\"reason\":\"Corrected time zone, year and swapped distance units.\"},{\"segment\":\"b1\",\"disposition\":\"superseded\",\"replacement\":\"# Voyager 2 at Uranus\",\"reason\":\"Identical title; the base document's copy is used.\"},{\"segment\":\"b2\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon count, names, author, tradition year and ring counts.\"},{\"segment\":\"b3\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 mph) and evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (497 miles) below the top cloud surface.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected wind speed, 'lake' to ocean, and depth figures.\"},{\"segment\":\"b5\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected moon names and 'smaller' to larger.\"},{\"segment\":\"b6\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly nine-year travels.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected mission duration.\"},{\"segment\":\"b9\",\"disposition\":\"reworded\",\"replacement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"reason\":\"Corrected crew count and accident date.\"}],\"mismatch\":\"\",\"additions\":[{\"statement\":\"Although Voyager 2 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the two planetary encounters, mission planners directed the veteran spacecraft to Uranus—a journey that would take about 4,5 years.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 3 had fulfilled its primary mission goals with the three planetary encounters\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"No Voyager 3 flew; Voyager 2's prior encounters were Jupiter and Saturn\"},{\"statement\":\"The Uranus encounter’s geometry was also defined by the possibility of a future encounter with Neptune: Voyager 2 had only 5.5 hours of close study during its flyby.\",\"corrects\":\"future encounter with Saturn: Voyager 1 had only 6.4 days\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Saturn preceded Uranus; Neptune followed; close study was about 5.5 hours\"},{\"statement\":\"The first human-made object to fly past Uranus, Voyager 2 began its long-range observations of the planet on Nov. 4, 1985, when signals took approximately 2,5 hours to reach Earth.\",\"corrects\":\"Voyager 1's short-range observations of the planet began Jan. 31, 1987\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Jan. 31, 1987 postdates the 1986 flyby; observations began Nov. 4, 1985\"},{\"statement\":\"Light conditions were 400 times less than terrestrial conditions.\",\"corrects\":\"five-hundred times less\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Uranus at ~19 AU gets roughly 1/370 of Earth's sunlight; NASA cites 400\"},{\"statement\":\"Closest approach to Uranus took place at 17:59 UT Jan. 24, 1986, at a range of about 81,500 kilometers (50,640 miles).\",\"corrects\":\"17:59 ED Jan. 24, 1968, at a range of about 50,640 kilometers (81,500 miles)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Flyby was in 1986 (UT); 81,500 km equals 50,640 miles, not the reverse\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"11 new moons (given such names as Pucka, Portila, Juliette, Kressida, Rosalinde, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelina, Ophelia, and Bianca II – obvious allusions to Goethe\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Ten moons were found, named for Shakespeare characters; the list names ten\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"begun in 1687\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Uranus was found in 1781; John Herschel named its moons in 1852\"},{\"statement\":\"During its flyby, Voyager 2 discovered 10 new moons (given such names as Puck, Portia, Juliet, Cressida, Rosalind, Belinda, Desdemona, Cordelia, Ophelia, and Bianca – obvious allusions to Shakespeare, continuing a naming tradition begun in 1852), two new rings in addition to the “older” nine rings, and a magnetic field tilted at 66 degrees off-axis and off-center.\",\"corrects\":\"three new rings in addition to the “older” eight rings\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Nine rings were known from 1977; Voyager 2 added two\"},{\"statement\":\"The spacecraft found wind speeds in Uranus’ atmosphere as high as 724 km/h (450 mph) and evidence of a boiling ocean of water some 800 kilometers (497 miles) below the top cloud surface.\",\"corrects\":\"450 km/h (72400 meters per hour) and found evidence of a boiling lake of water some 479 miles (900 kilometers)\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Winds reached 450 mph (724 km/h); the ocean lies 800 km (497 mi) down\"},{\"statement\":\"Voyager 2 also returned spectacular photos of Miranda, Oberon, Ariel, Umbriel, and Titania, five of Uranus’ larger moons.\",\"corrects\":\"Ariele, Umbrella, and Titan, five of Uranus’ smaller moons\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Titan orbits Saturn; Ariel, Umbriel, Titania are major Uranian moons\"},{\"statement\":\"In flying by Miranda at a range of only 17.560 miles (28.260 kilometers), the spacecraft came closest to any object so far in its nearly nine-year travels.\",\"corrects\":\"nearly century-long travels\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Launched in 1977, Voyager 2 had flown under nine years by 1986\"},{\"statement\":\"The spectacular news of the Uranus encounter was interrupted the same day by the tragic Challenger accident that killed seven astronauts during their space shuttle launch Jan. 28, 1986.\",\"corrects\":\"killed six astronauts during their space shuttle launch Feb. 28, 1986\",\"basis\":\"own-knowledge\",\"source\":\"\",\"reason\":\"Challenger broke up on Jan. 28, 1986, killing all seven crew\"}]}","ttft_ms":61358,"type":"result","duration_ms":88748,"uuid":"b3997a5d-4702-4928-b347-dac1e6c37cdf","ttft_stream_ms":5894,"time_to_request_ms":4562,"first_content_frame_ms":5895,"queued_turn_count":0,"result_index":0}
